#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087437 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e2458e5-8061-5f3f-9b4f-4d0bdd0ec641" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations Written in Standard Form"}] :question/id #uuid "5faa755c-1e2d-52a7-a521-49f79b94ec39" :question/math-academy-id "e-14709" :question/problem "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\n1. $-5x + y=3$\n2. $3x - y= -2$\n3. $3x= 5$\n4. $2x- 6y= 4$\n\nWhich of these lines is written in standard form?" :question/worked-solution "The *standard form* equation of a line is\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b$, and $c$ are all integers,\n- $a\\geq 0$, and\n- $a,b$, and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations.\n\n- Equation I is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-5$).\n- Equation II is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n$\\underbrace{3x- y}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{\\phantom{y}-2\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}$\nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $3$, $-1$, and $-2$) and don't have common factors greater than $1$. Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($3$).\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n$\\underbrace{3x}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{\\phantom{y}5\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}$\nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $3$ and $5$) and don't have common factors greater than $1$. Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($3$).\n- Equation IV is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(2,-6$, and $4)$ have a common factor of $2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087438 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087710 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f399f6ed-91bc-594e-9a40-b7c79f60c5dc" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Slope-Intercept Form to Standard Form"}] :question/id #uuid "7a4196bc-1f72-5435-9c00-c1fbef60b549" :question/math-academy-id "e-14710" :question/problem "Write $y = 7x+\\dfrac{2}{5}$ in standard form." :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form\n\n$$\nax+by = c\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$, and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and\n\n$$\na\\geq 0\n$$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 7x + \\frac{2}{5} \\\\\ny - 7x &= \\frac{2}{5} \\\\\n- 7x + y &= \\frac{2}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the equation is in the form\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nHowever, it violates the rule\n\n$$\na \\geq 0\n$$\n\nWe can fix this by multiplying the equation by $-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x + y &= \\frac{2}{5} \\\\\n(-1) \\cdot (-7x + y) &= (-1) \\cdot (\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\\n7x - y &= -\\frac{2}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $c$ is a fraction, not an integer. To fix this, we multiply by $5$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x - y &= -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\\n5 \\cdot (7x - y) &= 5 \\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\\n35x - 5y &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b$, and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and\n\n$$\na\\geq 0\n$$\n\nSo we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $35x-5y=-2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087439 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087711 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "daa37544-c4ec-5b8b-91a5-ccf5ee8f50d6" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Arbitrary Line to Standard Form"}] :question/id #uuid "9e0c4000-c24e-5761-8cbe-60699b557eca" :question/math-academy-id "e-14711" :question/problem "Write $y-1=x$ in standard form." :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form\n\n$$\nax+by = c\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$, and $c$ are integers with no common factors and\n\n$$\na\\geq 0\n$$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n-x+y=1\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x + y &= 1 \\\\\n(-1)(-x + y) &= -1 \\\\\nx - y &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b$, and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and\n\n$$\na \\geq 0\n$$\n\nSo we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $x-y=-1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087440 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087712 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefba93e-12c7-5835-80fb-b36463f140d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Equation of a Line Passing Through Two Points"}] :question/id #uuid "f78832f7-acd3-5779-b171-0650782b918e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14712" :question/problem "Calculate the equation of the straight line that passes through the points $(-1,3)$ and $(2,4)$. Write the answer in standard form." :question/worked-solution "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\nm = \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} = (4 - 3)/(2 - (-1)) = \\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(2,4)$. The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - 4 &= \\frac{1}{3}(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, we first multiply by $3$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - 4 &= \\frac{1}{3}(x - 2) \\\\\n3 \\cdot (y - 4) &= 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(x - 2) \\\\\n3y - 12 &= x - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3y - 12 &= x - 2 \\\\\n3y &= x + 10 \\\\\n- x + 3y &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x + 3y &= 10 \\\\\n(-1) \\cdot (-x + 3y) &= (-1) \\cdot 10 \\\\\nx - 3y &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is $x-3y = -10$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087712 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefba93e-12c7-5835-80fb-b36463f140d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Equation of a Line Passing Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "4bd3c904-aa13-5663-a037-27214c2b3f20" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085126} {:answer-field/id #uuid "5ac67115-1932-54f2-8277-c9e7f1701f81" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085127}] :question/id #uuid "2b068d36-9b51-5e2a-ba98-4d4ce9e05360" :question/math-academy-id "q-208786" :question/problem "Express the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-3,-2)$ and $(-1, 4)$ in standard form. {{field-1}}\n$=$\n{{field-2}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e2458e5-8061-5f3f-9b4f-4d0bdd0ec641" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations Written in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef77e145-5232-5daf-8b5f-0fb282cf9be6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085969} {:answer/id #uuid "31d269e4-a2d4-5b5c-85aa-76e93abcc2d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085970} {:answer/id #uuid "3551a33d-6664-58b2-9a9b-881dde1b9a0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085971} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0896f6-a2c8-565f-b6c2-08b572b5a62b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085972} {:answer/id #uuid "6e44c0d4-a88e-511b-a1ea-ce56e6698eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085973}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7f2db0c7-16fd-5c37-b274-a36499dcdd56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085968}] :question/id #uuid "079b3242-4c32-569f-b103-9bf2414bbafc" :question/math-academy-id "q-59668" :question/problem "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\n1. $2x = 3$\n2. $-x + 2y = 3$\n3. $x - 2y = 0$\n\nWhich of these lines is written in standard form?"}]}
